Template: homelab-dovecot/db_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PostgreSQL host to bootstrap the runtime role on:
 Used once, at install time, to create the narrow homelab_dovecot_runtime
 role and grant it column-scoped SELECT on api.users (see
 script/homelab-dovecot-bootstrap-role). Needs direct Postgres access
 (peer auth as the postgres OS user, locally or over SSH) — this is NOT
 the host Dovecot connects to at runtime, see pgbouncer_host below.

Template: homelab-dovecot/pgbouncer_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PgBouncer host for the runtime SQL passdb connection:
 Dovecot's own SQL passdb connects here at runtime (port 6432), never
 straight to Postgres — see CLAUDE.md's split runtime/migrate role
 design. The bootstrapped role is also registered into this host's
 pgbouncer userlist.txt.

Template: homelab-dovecot/advertise_host
Type: string
Default: 127.0.0.1
Description: This host's own address to advertise in the infrastructure topology registry:
 Written into homelab-api's api.infrastructure_registry (see
 `homelab-cli topology`) purely for operator visibility -- unlike the
 HTTP service registry, nothing forwards requests here based on this
 value. Leave as 127.0.0.1 only if this host has no other reachable
 address; set it to this host's real reachable address (e.g. its
 data-plane IP) for any distributed deployment, or the topology view
 will misleadingly show dovecot as running on loopback.

Template: homelab-dovecot/mail_domain
Type: string
Default:
Description: Primary mail domain this server handles (optional):
 Purely informational/logged — not enforced anywhere. Virtual users are
 identified by their full email address regardless of this value.

Template: homelab-dovecot/api_base
Type: string
Default: http://127.0.0.1:3000
Description: Base URL where homelab-api is reachable, for XOAUTH2/OAUTHBEARER:
 Written into the oauth2 passdb's introspection_url (.../api/v1/auth/
 introspect) — this is what lets Roundcube's SSO login (a homelab-sso-
 issued access token) actually authenticate over IMAP, alongside the
 existing SQL passdb for plain-password logins. Server-to-server, so
 this is normally a loopback address, not a public one.

Template: homelab-dovecot/behind_haproxy
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Put this Dovecot's IMAP/IMAPS traffic behind HAProxy (homelab-haproxy)?
 Recommended, and the default: matches this project's own production
 dovecot, which really is fronted by a real HAProxy this way (confirmed
 live), and it's the setup that stays correct whether this host runs
 alone or split from Postfix onto a different box -- nothing to
 reconfigure at the mail-transport layer either way. Set up
 homelab-haproxy in front of this host (locally, proxying to
 127.0.0.1, if this is a single-box install; on whichever host holds
 the public IP, if split) to actually receive connections once this is
 answered "true".
 .
 Answering "true" enables `haproxy = yes` on the imap-login listeners,
 which then REQUIRE a PROXY-protocol preamble on every connection --
 confirmed live: until HAProxy is actually in front of this host and
 sending that preamble (send-proxy on its backend line), every direct
 IMAP/IMAPS login is rejected outright. Answer "false" only for a
 deliberately direct, unproxied setup (e.g. while still setting up
 homelab-haproxy) -- dpkg-reconfigure homelab-dovecot to change this
 later either way, without a full reinstall.
